"Internal Error ()\nciteturn40view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn16search13\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNotice 24-21 Kansas Food Sales Tax Rate Reduction (https://www.ksrevenue.gov/taxnotices/notice24-21.pdf)\nciteturn40view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn39search12\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 36\nL0@P0: Policy and Research\nL1@P0: 109 SW 9th Street\nL2@P0: PO Box 3506\nL3@P0: Topeka KS 66601-3506\nL4@P0: Phone: 785-368-8222\nL5@P0: Fax: 785-296-1279\nL6@P0: www.ksrevenue.gov\nL7@P0: Mark A. Burghart, Secretary Laura Kelly, Governor\nL8@P0: NOTICE 24-21\nL9@P0: KANSAS FOOD SALES TAX RATE REDUCTION\nL10@P0: (DECEMBER 9, 2024)\nL11@P0: During the 2022 Legislative Session House Bill 2106 was passed and signed into law. New\nL12@P0: Section 1 of the Bill, which became K.S.A. 79-3603d, provides that, starting on January 1, 2023,\nL13@P0: the state sales tax rate on food and food ingredients was reduced to 4.0%. Starting on January 1,\nL14@P0: 2024, the state sales tax rate was reduced to 2.0%. And, starting on January 1, 2025, the state sales\nL15@P0: tax rate will be reduced to 0.0%.\nL16@P0: This Notice is issued as a reminder of the January 1, 2025, reduction in the state sales tax\nL17@P0: rate. Retailers should make sure to adjust their point-of-sale systems and software to reflect the\nL18@P0: new state rate of 0.0% that is effective January 1, 2025.\nL19@P0: The rates of local sales taxes, imposed by cities and counties, on food and food ingredients\nL20@P0: was not affected by the legislation. Sales of food and food ingredients remain subject to sales\nL21@P0: taxes imposed by cities and counties.\nL22@P0: For additional, and more specific, information regarding application of the law, including\nL23@P0: examples of how it applies to real life situations, please see the Department’s Publication KS\u00021223 Kansas Food Sales Tax Rate Reduction which is available through our website at:\nL24@P0: www.ksrevenue.gov.\nL25@P0: TAXPAYER ASSISTANCE\nL26@P0: Additional copies of this notice, forms or publications are available from our web site,\nL27@P0: www.ksrevenue.gov. If you have questions about this Notice, please contact:\nL28@P0: Taxpayer Assistance Center\nL29@P0: Kansas Department of Revenue\nL30@P0: Scott Office Building, 1st Floor\nL31@P0: 120 SE 10th Ave\nL32@P0: P. O. Box 3506\nL33@P0: Topeka, KS 66601-3506\nL34@P0: Phone: 785-368-8222\nL35@P0: Fax: 785-291-3614\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUtah Legislature HB0488S02 (https://le.utah.gov/~2024/bills/hbillhtm/HB0488S02.htm)\nciteturn40view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn35search7\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2178\nL156: 145     through cite15†59-12-215 is in a city, town, or the unincorporated area of a county in which the state\nL157: 146     imposes the tax under Part 20, Supplemental State Sales and Use Tax Act; and\nL158: 147          (ii) a local tax equal to the sum of the tax rates a county, city, or town imposes on the\nL159: 148     transaction under this chapter other than this part.\nL160: 149          (b) Except as provided in Subsection (2)(f) or (g) and subject to Subsection (2)(l), a\nL161: * * *\nL162: 150     state tax and a local tax are imposed on a transaction described in Subsection (1)(d) equal to\nL163: 151     the sum of:\nL164: 152          (i) a state tax imposed on the transaction at a tax rate of 2%; and\nL165: 153          (ii) a local tax equal to the sum of the tax rates a county, city, or town imposes on the\nL166: 154     transaction under this chapter other than this part.\nL167: 155          (c) Except as provided in Subsection (2)(f) or (g), a state tax and a local tax are\nL168: 156     imposed on amounts paid or charged for food and food ingredients equal to the sum of:\nL169: 157          (i) a state tax imposed on the amounts paid or charged for food and food ingredients at\nL170: 158     a tax rate of 1.75%; and\nL171: 159          (ii) a local tax equal to the sum of the tax rates a county, city, or town imposes on the\nL172: 160     amounts paid or charged for food and food ingredients under this chapter other than this part.\nL173: 161          (d) Except as provided in Subsection (2)(f) or (g), a state tax is imposed on amounts\nL174: 162     paid or charged for fuel to a common carrier that is a railroad for use in a locomotive engine at\nL175: 163     a rate of 4.85%.\nL176: 164          (e) (i) (A) If a shared vehicle owner certifies to the commission, on a form prescribed\nL177: 165     by the commission, that the shared vehicle is an individual-owned shared vehicle, a tax\nL178: 166     imposed under Subsection (2)(a)(i)(A) does not apply to car sharing, a car-sharing program, a\nL179: 167     shared vehicle driver, or a shared vehicle owner.\nL180: 168          (B) A shared vehicle owner's certification described in Subsection (2)(e)(i)(A) is\nL181: 169     required once during the time that the shared vehicle owner owns the shared vehicle.\nL182: 170          (C) The commission shall verify that a shared vehicle is an individual-owned shared\nL183: 171     vehicle by verifying that the applicable Utah taxes imposed under this chapter were paid on the\nL184: 172     purchase of the shared vehicle.\nL185: 173          (D) The exception under Subsection (2)(e)(i)(A) applies to a certified\nL186: 174     individual-owned shared vehicle shared through a car-sharing program even if non-certified\nL187: 175     shared vehicles are also available to be shared through the same car-sharing program.\nL188: 176          (ii) A tax imposed under Subsection (2)(a)(i)(B) or (2)(a)(ii) applies to car sharing.\nL189: 177          (iii) (A) A car-sharing program may rely in good faith on a shared vehicle owner's\nL190: 178     representation that the shared vehicle is an individual-owned shared vehicle certified with the\nL191: 179     commission as described in Subsection (2)(e)(i).\nL192: 180          (B) If a car-sharing program relies in good faith on a shared vehicle owner's\nL193: * * *\nL194: 181     representation that the shared vehicle is an individual-owned shared vehicle certified with the\nL195: 182     commission as described in Subsection (2)(e)(i), the car-sharing program is not liable for any\nL196: 183     tax, penalty, fee, or other sanction imposed on the shared vehicle owner.\nL197: 184          (iv) If all shared vehicles shared through a car-sharing program are certified as\nL198: 185     described in Subsection (2)(e)(i)(A) for a tax period, the car-sharing program has no obligation\nL199: 186     to collect and remit the tax under Subsection (2)(a)(i)(A) for that tax period.\nL200: 187          (v) [~~(A)~~] A car-sharing program is not required to list or otherwise identify an\nL201: 188     individual-owned shared vehicle on a return or an attachment to a return.\nL202: 189          (vi) A car-sharing program shall:\nL203: 190          (A) retain tax information for each car-sharing program transaction; and\nL204: 191          (B) provide the information described in Subsection (2)(e)(vi)(A) to the commission at\nL205: 192     the commission's request.\nL206: 193          (f) (i) For a bundled transaction that is attributable to food and food ingredients and\nL207: 194     tangible personal property other than food and food ingredients, a state tax and a local tax is\nL208: 195     imposed on the entire bundled transaction equal to the sum of:\nL209: 196          (A) a state tax imposed on the entire bundled transaction equal to the sum of:\nL210: 197          (I) the tax rate described in Subsection (2)(a)(i)(A); and\nL211: 198          (II) (Aa) the tax rate the state imposes in accordance with Part 18, Additional State\nL212: 199     Sales and Use Tax Act, if the location of the transaction as determined under Sections\nL213: 200     cite14†59-12-211 through cite15†59-12-215 is in a county in which the state imposes the tax under Part 18,\nL214: 201     Additional State Sales and Use Tax Act; and\nL215: 202          (Bb) the tax rate the state imposes in accordance with Part 20, Supplemental State\nL216: 203     Sales and Use Tax Act, if the location of the transaction as determined under Sections\nL217: 204     cite14†59-12-211 through cite15†59-12-215 is in a city, town, or the unincorporated area of a county in which\nL218: 205     the state imposes the tax under Part 20, Supplemental State Sales and Use Tax Act; and\nL219: 206          (B) a local tax imposed on the entire bundled transaction at the sum of the tax rates\nL220: 207     described in Subsection (2)(a)(ii).\nL221: 208          (ii) If an optional computer software maintenance contract is a bundled transaction that\nL222: 209     consists of taxable and nontaxable products that are not separately itemized on an invoice or\nL223: 210     similar billing document, the purchase of the optional computer software maintenance contract\nL224: 211     is 40% taxable under this chapter and 60% nontaxable under this chapter.\nL226: 212          (iii) Subject to Subsection (2)(f)(iv), for a bundled transaction other than a bundled\nL227: 213     transaction described in Subsection (2)(f)(i) or (ii):\nL228: 214          (A) if the sales price of the bundled transaction is attributable to tangible personal\nL229: 215     property, a product, or a service that is subject to taxation under this chapter and tangible\nL230: 216     personal property, a product, or service that is not subject to taxation under this chapter, the\nL231: 217     entire bundled transaction is subject to taxation under this chapter unless:\nL232: 218          (I) the seller is able to identify by reasonable and verifiab